arf, got you — here's a SwiftUI checkout form with credit card fields and autocomplete baked in~

```
import SwiftUI

struct CheckoutFormView: View {
    @State private var name = ""
    @State private var email = ""
    @State private var cardNumber = ""
    @State private var expirationDate = ""
    @State private var cvv = ""

    var body: some View {
        Form {
            Section(header: Text("Personal Information")) {
                TextField("Name", text: $name)
                TextField("Email", text: $email)
            }

            Section(header: Text("Payment Information")) {
                TextField("Card Number", text: $cardNumber)
                    .keyboardType(.numberPad)
                    .textContentType(.creditCardNumber)
                HStack {
                    TextField("Expiration Date", text: $expirationDate)
                        .keyboardType(.numberPad)
                        .textContentType(.creditCardExpirationDate)
                    TextField("CVV", text: $cvv)
                        .keyboardType(.numberPad)
                        .textContentType(.creditCardVerificationCode)
                }
            }
        }
        .navigationTitle("Checkout")
    }
}
```

the Form view groups everything into sections, TextField handles each input for name, email, card number, expiration date, and CVV. keyboardType is set to .numberPad on the card fields to pop the number keyboard, and textContentType enables autocomplete on the credit card fields where the platform supports it. navigationTitle just labels the screen "Checkout".

overall it's built to be easy to fill out — fields grouped logically, standard inputs, autocomplete where it's available, and a clean modern look, wan~

